Use table-driven tests when cases share a contract, and regression tests for
observable failures. Use `t.TempDir`, `t.Cleanup`, local test servers, and explicit
synchronization instead of live dependencies or sleeps as proof of ordering.
Test cancellation, deadlines, concurrent access, and cleanup when relevant.
Race checks complement assertions; a passing run is not proof of all schedules.
Use fuzz tests for parsers and boundary-heavy inputs when they add useful coverage.

Keep public examples compiling and use executable examples for API usage when
appropriate. Preserve exported APIs, error semantics, and wire formats under the
project's compatibility policy. Update maintained docs and generated references
with behavior changes, using existing generation commands. Run checks proportional
to the change and report actual results and unsupported environments.

